{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "meta": {
    "id": "quiz_modes-ch3-checkpoint",
    "slug": "modes-ch3-checkpoint",
    "title": "The Major Family",
    "summary": "Eight questions on the one home chord and its two dials, the avoid note, Lydian's raised fourth, Mixolydian's flattened seventh, and what a window's edges do to a diagram.",
    "kind": "checkpoint",
    "passThresholdPct": 70
  },
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q_modes-ch3-checkpoint.the-family",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "Lydian, Ionian and Mixolydian all share one home chord. Which chord is it, and which two degrees are the dials that separate the three?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " (" },
            { "text": "A C# E", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": "), and the " },
            { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and the " },
            { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " (" },
            { "text": "A C# E", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": "), and the " },
            { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and the " },
            { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Am", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " (" },
            { "text": "A C E", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": "), and the " },
            { "text": "6", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and the " },
            { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " (" },
            { "text": "A C# E", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": "), and the " },
            { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and the " },
            { "text": "6", "marks": ["code"] }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        { "text": "Lydian is " },
        { "text": "#4 7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", Ionian is " },
        { "text": "4 7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", Mixolydian is " },
        { "text": "4 b7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — one home chord and two switches. The " },
        { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " never moves inside this family; if it did, the home chord would turn minor, which is where the family ends. The "
        },
        { "text": "Am", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " answer, with the " },
        { "text": "6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and the " },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", describes the other family exactly — it is just not this one. And the " },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and the " },
        { "text": "6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " are natural in all three of these, so neither can tell them apart." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_modes-ch3-checkpoint.no-accent",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "Ionian's scale card lights no tinted chip and its CAGED windows outline no dot. Why not?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A characteristic note is whatever separates a scale from its nearest plain relative, and Ionian is the plain relative"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Ionian's characteristic note is the " },
            { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", but a leading tone is never tinted" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Ionian shares too many notes with Lydian and Mixolydian for any single one of them to be worth marking"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [{ "text": "The tint is only ever drawn for a flattened degree" }]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "It is a fact about Ionian, not a gap in the drawing: a characteristic note names whatever sets a scale apart from its plain relative, and Ionian is the thing the rest of this family is set apart from. Aeolian carries none for the same reason, one family down. The "
        },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " cannot be it, because Lydian has one too — a note shared with a neighbour separates you from nothing. And a raised degree gets tinted like any other: Lydian's "
        },
        { "text": "#4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is outlined violet." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_modes-ch3-checkpoint.avoid-note",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        { "text": "What makes the " },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " an avoid note over a static " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " chord?" }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "It sits a half step above the " },
            { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
            {
              "text": ", which is a note of the chord — so it fights the chord if you land on it and hold it"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It is not one of the chord's three notes, and any note outside the chord clashes with it"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "It is a note you must not play at all while an " },
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " chord is sounding" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "It sits a half step below the " },
            { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", which is a note of the chord" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        { "text": "The chord is " },
        { "text": "A C# E", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and the " },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is " },
        { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", one fret above the " },
        { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". Being outside the chord is not enough on its own — " },
        { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "F#", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " are outside it too and each sits a whole step above a chord tone, so neither fights. Nothing is forbidden either: passing through the "
        },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is fine, landing and staying on it is not. And a half step " },
        { "text": "below", "marks": ["italic"] },
        { "text": " a chord tone leans up into it rather than grinding — " },
        { "text": "G#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " under the root does exactly that. In any case the " },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is a whole step below the " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", not a half." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_modes-ch3-checkpoint.lydian-consonance",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        { "text": "Over a static " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " chord, Lydian's " },
        { "text": "#4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " (" },
        { "text": "D#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ") causes none of the trouble Ionian's " },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " (" },
        { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ") does. Why — and where did listeners actually place Lydian?" }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "D#", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " is a whole step above the " },
            { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and a half step below the " },
            { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
            {
              "text": ", so it sits a half step above nothing in the chord — and yet listeners judged Ionian happier, Lydian landing third of the six tested"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "D#", "marks": ["code"] },
            {
              "text": " is outside the chord, so the ear takes it as a passing note wherever it falls — and listeners judged Lydian the happiest of the six, exactly as the ladder predicts"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "D#", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " is the same distance from the " },
            { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " as " },
            { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
            {
              "text": " is, but it resolves upward instead — and listeners could not separate Lydian from Ionian at all"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Lydian has no notes outside the " },
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " chord at all — and listeners judged it the happiest of the six" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        { "text": "Nothing in " },
        { "text": "A C# E", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " sits a half step under " },
        { "text": "D#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", so there is nothing for it to grind against — while " },
        { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " sits one fret over the " },
        { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". That is the whole difference, and it is the real reason Lydian is worth knowing. The happiness answers are the trap: the ladder puts Lydian at the top and the listeners did not, placing it third at "
        },
        { "text": ".58", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " behind Ionian's " },
        { "text": ".83", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " — a difference that did reach significance, so \"couldn't tell them apart\" is wrong too. And a seven-note scale always has four notes outside a three-note chord."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_modes-ch3-checkpoint.lydian-vamp",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        { "text": "The Lydian vamp is " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "–" },
        { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", a " },
        { "text": "I", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "–" },
        { "text": "II", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". What is the " },
        { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " chord doing there?" }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " is " },
            { "text": "B D# F#", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", so it carries the " },
            { "text": "#4", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " — and it fits inside Lydian and inside none of the other six modes on " },
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " is " },
            { "text": "B D# F#", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", so it carries the " },
            { "text": "#4", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " — and it shares a note with the " },
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " chord, which is what holds the pair together" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " is the " },
            { "text": "V", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " of " },
            { "text": "E", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " major, Lydian's parent, so the vamp names the parent scale" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " is " },
            { "text": "B D F#", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", so it carries the " },
            { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        { "text": "Ionian needs a " },
        { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " where " },
        { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " needs a " },
        { "text": "D#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", so the second chord asserts the " },
        { "text": "#4", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " and rules the neighbour out in one gesture. The pair shares no note at all — "
        },
        { "text": "A C# E", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " against " },
        { "text": "B D# F#", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ", two major triads a whole tone apart with nothing in common, which is exactly why the loop sounds like nothing else in this pathway. Naming the parent scale is the habit this pathway tells you to put down once it has found you the notes. And "
        },
        { "text": "B D F#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is " },
        { "text": "Bm", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", a different chord." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_modes-ch3-checkpoint.mixolydian-seventh",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        { "text": "What actually connects A Mixolydian to the chord " },
        { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "?" }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Mixolydian's one tritone falls between the " },
            { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and the " },
            { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " — " },
            { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and " },
            { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " — and those are the two notes inside " },
            { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " that make it what it is" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Both contain a " },
            { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", and that shared note is the whole of the connection" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " is the " },
            { "text": "V", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " of " },
            { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " major, which is Mixolydian's parent scale" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Mixolydian's tritone falls between the " },
            { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and the " },
            { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", the two notes " },
            { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " adds to the " },
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " triad" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is " },
        { "text": "A C# E G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and its " },
        { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " are the mode's own tritone — so the chord and the mode are one idea rather than two things that happen to agree. Sharing a "
        },
        { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " is true but shallow: Dorian, Aeolian, Phrygian and Locrian all have one and none of them is the "
        },
        { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " sound. Naming the parent is a way of finding notes, not of explaining a sound. And "
        },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is Ionian's tritone — " },
        { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " adds exactly one note to the triad, the " },
        { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_modes-ch3-checkpoint.window-edges",
      "kind": "multi-select",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "Which of these are true when one degree of the scale inside a CAGED window moves a fret?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The window itself does not move — it is a fixed span of frets anchored on the root"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Raising a degree moves its dot up a fret, so a dot sitting on the window's top fret raises out of the picture"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Two diagrams can hold the same number of dots and still be different pictures"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [{ "text": "Counting the dots tells you which scale is filling the window" }]
        },
        {
          "id": "e",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Flattening a degree shifts the whole window down a fret to follow it" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerIds": ["a", "b", "c"],
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "A form is a fret span anchored on the root, so nothing shifts it. The E form at frets 4 to 8 holds seventeen dots for "
        },
        { "text": "A Major", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and eighteen for " },
        { "text": "A Lydian", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": "; the G form at frets 1 to 5 holds seventeen for both, and they are still different pictures. And the A form at the nut runs twelve for "
        },
        { "text": "A Lydian", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", thirteen for " },
        { "text": "A Major", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and fifteen for " },
        { "text": "A Mixolydian", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " — the same one-degree change losing dots in one direction and gaining them in the other, purely because that window is clamped at fret 0. The dot count is a fact about the frame, not about the mode."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_modes-ch3-checkpoint.lean-on-one-note",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        { "text": "A loop rocks between " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " and refuses to resolve anywhere else. Which note do you lean on to make it sound like the mode it is asking for — and what happens if you lean on "
        },
        { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " instead?" }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Lean on " },
            { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", the " },
            { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " — Mixolydian's characteristic note. Leaning on " },
            { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " puts you on the avoid note, a half step above the " },
            { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " chord's third" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Lean on " },
            { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", the " },
            { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
            {
              "text": " — it belongs to both Ionian and Mixolydian, so it is always the safe choice"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Lean on " },
            { "text": "D#", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", the " },
            { "text": "#4", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " — a second chord a whole tone from home is always carrying it" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            { "text": "Lean on " },
            { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": ", the " },
            { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " — it is the note that separates this family from the minor one" }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is " },
        { "text": "G B D", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and the " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " in it is the " },
        { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — the note the whole mode turns on. Sitting on the " },
        { "text": "D", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " is the trap: being in the scale is not the same as being safe over the chord, and a note shared with the neighbouring mode cannot tell you which mode you are in either way. "
        },
        { "text": "D#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " belongs to the other vamp, " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "–" },
        { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and is not in this loop at all: the second chord here sits a whole tone " },
        { "text": "below", "marks": ["italic"] },
        { "text": " home rather than above it. And " },
        { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " is shared by all three modes in this chapter, so leaning on it cannot single one out."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
